> 
> >Kevin Wayne wrote:
> >>
> >> Soldier ants seem to be able to do serious damage against ACs several
> >> points below zero. My samurai just dealt with the Bigroom, having to
> >> run back upstairs every few turns, even at AC -4 iirc, with a cloak of
> >> displacement, and most of the damage seemed to be coming from soldier
> >> ants. I'm not sure where the cutoff point is...
> >
> >       There's no cutoff.
> >
> >       I once spent a long time cranking through how effective
> >negative ACs were.  Rules of thumb:  if a monster's attack averages
> >N points of damage, then an AC of -N cuts the damage done in
> >half, and an AC of -2N cuts it to a quarter.
> >
> >       Soldier ants have two attacks:  2d4 3d4P, which average 5
> >and 7.5.  Split the difference, and an AC of -6 will take off about
> >half the damage from successful attacks.
> 
> So an AC of -12 would take off three-quarters of the damage, meaning
> about 3 hp damage per ant per turn, assuming both attacks hit, right?

Pretty much correct, i think. However, below-zero ACs are randomised
for purposes of determining chance-to-hit and damage reduction, so
even with the best possible AC, there's a chance your AC is considered
-1, meaning full or nearly-full damage and a good chance to be hit. At
-10 AC, soldier ants will _on average_ hit only rarely and for little
damage, but you still have a small chance to take 15+ points of damage
from a single ant in a single round.

> That's sort of what I meant by "cutoff point." When you have over 100
> hp and you're only taking 1-2 hp damage per turn (even if all the
> attacks do hit), the danger is relatively negligable.

I find that a poison-resistant character with -5 AC and 60 hp can
survive well against soldier ants[1], and one with -10 AC and 80+ hp
will consider them a mere nuisance.

[1] when facing one ant at a time and enough time to heal up after
dealing with the critter; when you end up in the middle of an ant
pack, you will still have to get out instantly.

